    <DataNode elementId="CPD-21554" textLabel="strychnine" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="QMGVPVSNSZLJIA-FVWCLLPLSA-O" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="500" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, hawkins, SRI, caspi">The genus |FRAME: TAX-26496| is the most important genus of the |FRAME: TAX-26468| family, which comprises approximately 190 species of trees and lianas. |FRAME: CPD-21554 Strychnine| is a terpene indole alkaloid belonging to the Strychnos family of Corynanthe alkaloids and was the first alkaloid to be identified in plants of the |FRAME: TAX-26496| genus. It is found in the seeds and bark of many plants in this genus. |FRAME: TAX-28545| is a tree native to the tropical forests on the Malabar Coast in Southern India, Sri Lanka and Indonesia. It has an orange-colored fruit that contains five seeds. The toxic effects of these seeds have been well known from the times of ancient India, although the chemical compound itself was not identified. In 1819, Pelletier and Caventou reported the isolation of strychnine, in pure form, as the principal toxin from |FRAME: TAX-28545| and |FRAME: TAX-1037790| |CITS: [Pelletier19]|. The correct structure was reported in 1946 |CITS: [Briggs46][Holmes46]|. The absolute stereochemistry of strychnine was established by Peerdeman in 1956 with X-ray crystallographic analysis |CITS: [Peerdeman56]|. Strychnine is one of the most complex natural products of its size, with twenty-four skeletal atoms arranged in seven rings, resulting in six contiguous stereogenic centers. Strychnine is a powerful toxin (a lethal dose of strychnine for an adult human is in the range of 30-100 mg). It is an antagonist of glycine and acetylcholine receptors and affects primarily the motor nerve fibers in the spinal cord which control muscle contraction. It binds noncovalently to the same receptor as glycine, preventing the inhibitory effects of glycine on the postsynaptic neuron and resulting in activation of the motor neurons. This activation causes spastic muscle contractions and eventually death by asphyxiation.</Comment>
